<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Comprehensive geriatric assessment, adapted for deafblindness, for a 78-year-old woman after a fall at home. Please look for any contributor we have missed and advise on fall prevention, cognitive baseline, and planning ahead. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Routine, within two weeks; please book a qualified tactile interpreter (the clinic will arrange and pay) and a double-length appointment. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>Is there any medical contributor to the fall beyond the hazard and reduced leg strength and balance?</item><item>How can cognition be screened validly when standard tools depend on vision or hearing, and what is her baseline?</item><item>What fall-prevention program and home changes do you advise, adapted to tactile communication?</item><item>What should be in place now so that her communication and wishes are respected if she becomes ill or is admitted to hospital?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Ms. Keller is seen at the request of Dr. Strand, through a qualified tactile interpreter, in a double-length appointment; she answered for herself throughout. She missed the last step where the back-step handrail ends three weeks ago and sat down hard without injury; she had no warning symptoms. She has had no other falls in the past year. She notices that rising from low chairs takes more effort than it did a few years ago. She has no urinary incontinence, no nocturia beyond once, no weight loss, and no change in the feeling in her fingers or feet, which she checks closely because she reads and receives language by touch. She sleeps seven hours, drinks a glass of wine with dinner a few times a week, and takes no medicines. She is independent in all activities of daily living; her assistant drives, shops, and reads her print mail. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Helen Keller was born on June 27, 1880, in Tuscumbia, Alabama. [D]</item><item>In February 1882, at 19 months, she had a febrile illness that her doctor, as she later wrote, called acute congestion of the stomach and brain and thought she would not survive; the fever left suddenly, but she never saw or heard again. [D]</item><item>The cause of the 1882 illness is unknown; scarlet fever, meningitis, and rubella have been proposed. [R]</item><item>From March 1887 her teacher, a graduate of the Perkins School for the Blind, taught her language by spelling words into her hand; she learned the manual alphabet and raised print for blind readers, and began to learn speech at 10. [D]</item><item>In her own account, others spoke to her by spelling into her hand with the single-hand manual alphabet while she rested her hand lightly on theirs; she spoke for herself, read the lips of some people by touch, and read and wrote braille. [D]</item><item>In 1904 she graduated cum laude from Radcliffe College, the first deafblind person to earn a college degree. [D]</item><item>From 1924 she worked as a public spokesperson for the American Foundation for the Blind for the rest of her life, and from 1946 she made world tours to 35 countries in 11 years on behalf of people with disabilities. [D]</item><item>A receipt dated September 1911 records the purchase of an artificial eye for her. [D]</item><item>She lived at Arcan Ridge in Easton, Connecticut, from the late 1930s for the rest of her life. [D]</item><item>Her father died in 1896 and her mother in 1921. [D]</item></list><paragraph>2 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

