<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Fall at home with increased fall risk (Z91.81). A mechanical fall at an environmental hazard, made more probable by reduced hip and knee strength and balance. There is no orthostatic hypotension, no medicine, no neuropathy, no cognitive impairment, and no continence or nutritional problem. Vision is not a modifiable factor here; she moves by touch, rails, and a sighted guide. [S]</item><item>Cognitive screening. Standard screening tools depend on vision or hearing, and none is validated for people who are deafblind. A tactile interview with fingerspelled word recall, spoken verbal fluency, and serial sevens, interpreted by a qualified tactile interpreter, shows no impairment. Use the same method each year so that any change is measured against her own baseline. [S]</item><item>Lifelong deafblindness (H54.0X55). Touch is her main channel for language and reading, and it is fully intact. Her greatest risks in illness are communication barriers: being examined without warning, consent taken without a qualified interpreter, or isolation in hospital. [S]</item><item>Bone health. Low bone mass with FRAX risk below the treatment thresholds; medicine is not indicated. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Intake fall assessment (Timed Up and Go, chair stand, balance), orthostatic vital signs, hemoglobin, metabolic panel, vitamin D, B12, and PHQ-9; today&apos;s bone density scan, which shows low bone mass (femoral neck T-score -1.9, spine -1.4) with a FRAX risk below treatment thresholds. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Home physical therapy for progressive strength and balance training twice a week for 12 weeks, then a daily home program; a qualified tactile interpreter at the first sessions and whenever new exercises are taught. [S]</item><item>Occupational therapy home visit: handrails on both sides of every step to the bottom step, tactile edge strips, clear routes, vibrating smoke and carbon monoxide alarms with a bed shaker, and a wearable alert button. [S]</item><item>Repeat Timed Up and Go, chair stand, and balance tests after the exercise program. [S]</item><item>No vitamin D supplement for fall prevention, since her level is adequate; dietary calcium of about 1,200 mg a day. [S]</item><item>Repeat the tactile cognitive check yearly at the wellness visit by the same method. [S]</item><item>Write a hospital communication passport in braille and print with her, and record her health care agent and wishes; review them at her annual wellness visit. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Strand today. The plan was given to Ms. Keller in braille, and she agreed with it. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Geriatric medicine will see her again after any further fall, a hospital admission, or a change in cognition or function; otherwise primary care continues the plan. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
